{"id":"rpk","slug":"rpk","name":"RPK","aliases":["RPKS","RPKN","RPKM","RPK74","RPKSN","LMG-F","RPK 74","RPKS74","RPK-16","RPKS-74","RPK-74N","RPK-74M","RPKS-74N","Molot Vepr","Arsenal LMG","Arsenal LMG-F","RPK machine gun","Ручной пулемёт Калашникова","Ruchnoy Pulemyot Kalashnikova"],"kind":"firearm","category":"machine-guns","subcategory":"Soviet light support weapon and variants","description":"The RPK is a Soviet 7.62×39mm light machine gun/squad automatic weapon that was developed by Mikhail Kalashnikov in the early 1960s, in parallel with the AKM assault rifle. The RPK was created to standardize the small arms inventory of the Soviet Army, where it replaced the 7.62×39mm RPD machine gun.","development":"The RPK is chambered in 7.62×39mm intermediate cartridge. It uses a standard AKM pistol grip and can also use standard AKM detachable box magazines, but it is most commonly used with a 40-round box magazine or a 75-round drum magazine. The U-shaped receiver is stamped from a smooth 1.5 mm (0.06 in) sheet of steel, compared with the 1 mm (0.039 in) sheet metal receiver used on the standard AKM. It features a thick laminated wood foregrip and a fixed laminated wood \"club-foot\" buttstock similar to the stock used on the RPD, which is designed to allow the user to fire from the prone position more comfortably. Interchangeability of parts between the RPK and AKM are moderate.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"7.62×39mm M43"},{"label":"Action","value":"Gas-operated, rotating bolt; closed bolt"},{"label":"Rate of Fire","value":"600 rounds/min","numeric":600,"unit":"rounds/min"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"745 m/s","numeric":745,"unit":"m/s"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"30-, 40-round box magazine, 75-round drum magazine"},{"label":"Sights (Iron sights)","value":"semi-shrouded front post and rear sliding tangent with an adjustable notch"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","val